In article <7109@cbnewsh.ATT.COM> wcs@cbnewsh.ATT.COM (Bill Stewart 201-949-0705 erebus.att.com!wcs) writes:
>
>  			[...]
>
>Powerful Spellbooks generally make things too easy;
>why mess around with a wand of polymorph when you can use a blessed
>spellbook that only costs 5 magic-points per zap?

Do you play a version with HARD defined? If so, you should find that your
high-level spells run out plenty fast (considerably less charges than your 
wand, on average). Also spell-casting is not possible when hungry or weakened.
Spells are more likely to fail, as success is affected by your stats (unlike
wands), and make you hungry. But I agree, power points are an almost 
meaningless constraint now that spell-casting has been de-emphasized.

>Aside from the problems described earlier in this discussion,
>you get jaded from carrying around all those luckstones and
>dilithium crystals you made out of piles of rock.

It takes a *lot* of polymorphing to get dilithium crystals (or it used to
in version 1.3 - the version that I won repeatedly when I first got addicted).
I think the theory is that the player will starve in that time. Of course,
that doesn't work because the player can throw corpses on the polymorph pile,
and remove the food rations into which they change (which are usually much
more nutritious than the corpses). If you have polymorphs to burn, it doesn't 
take long to amass dozens of food rations.

Remember that more than one luckstone doesn't give you more luck.

>Another unexpected easiness is the spellbook of create monster -
>you sit in a room with the right altar and create monsters to sacrifice,
>eat a few monsters as needed, pray every 300-odd turns, and watch
>the Ghods hand over blessings and artifacts.  Greed city.

Created monsters should, IMHO, be flagged in such a way that the gods don't
count them as "real" sacrifices. Either that, or the monster generation for 
create monster should always produce something that will be a challenge to kill
(i.e. starting players would get multiple hobgoblins, gnomes, etc., 
mega-characters would get minotaurs, guardian nagas, maybe powerful demons). I 
also think there should be diminishing returns from the gods - i.e. once you 
get an artifact, it should be *real* hard to get anything from them for a long 
time. And you should get hungry every time the gods "do" something to you, like 
raise your luck. Pretty soon you'll be forced to go to another level looking 
for food (unless you can go on a polymorph spree). Maybe altars should just
go *POOF* when the gods give you something good (with some random element,
perhaps?)

>What I tend to wish for, or get by whatever means, are a
>blessed spellbook of identify and a ring of conflict.
>Identify lets you identify EVERYTHING in one swell foop, and is
>probably too powerful - perhaps it should work like blessed scrolls
>of identify, which identify a small random number of things.

Hmmm... I'm surprised that this is the case, but I just tested it. I'll
check why this was changed, since I know it wasn't the case in older 
versions: there is a remote possibilty that this may be a bug, but the code
looks pretty deliberate. On the other hand, what would be the difference 
between casting the identify spell several times and identifying everything 
at once, except a slight time delay (assuming, as above, that your spells 
don't burn out, and you have plenty of power points with which to cast them)?

>Conflict, of course, makes it feasible to deal with large rooms of
>nasties without needing a lot of time or an immense armor class.

This is one of my gripes, too (although bear in mind that monsters killed by 
other monsters don't give you any XP or treasure). But I haven't thought of 
any good way to make it more reasonable. Ideas, people?

The game is noticeably easier as of PL6, but not what I would call a giveaway.
The above are just some spots that I personally believe are out of balance with
the rest of the game. (Almost sounds like a disclaimer, huh?)
